<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<!--
  N01 Incompatible Rule

  MUST BE REFUSED. The rule Gorp.rtf opens with: a comparison across quantities
  that do not share a dimension — seconds against kilograms. Everything else in
  this design is clean, so the only finding is the one being tested.

  A UNIT TEST DESIGN THAT IS SUPPOSED TO FAIL. Bundled and read-only:
  suite [25] asserts the check CATCHES this, so a fix here would quietly
  remove the test.
-->
<gorp name="N01 Incompatible Rule">
  <!-- nos-uml-state {"mode":"ai-design","title":"N01 Incompatible Rule","layout":{"ut.bad.Mix":{"x":60,"y":60,"w":280,"h":140},"ut.bad.Compare":{"x":420,"y":60,"w":280,"h":140},"ut.bad.Verdict":{"x":780,"y":60,"w":280,"h":140}}} -->

  <comment view="class">MUST BE REFUSED. mass carries a validate rule comparing elapsed against mass — seconds against kilograms, which share no dimension.

Everything else here is clean on purpose, so the only thing Check can find is the thing being tested. This is the case Gorp.rtf opens with; T03 is the same shape with both sides in metres.</comment>
  <expect>Check must report ONE finding and it must name Mix.mass: the two sides of the rule are not compatible quantities. Run must be greyed out while it stands.

A green check on this design means the unit comparison stopped working.</expect>
  <model namespace="ut.bad.Mix">
    <variables>
      <variable name="elapsed" type="double" collapse="Monotone"><unit basis="\second" namespace="D-SI"/></variable>
      <variable name="mass"    type="double" collapse="Monotone"><unit basis="\kilogram" namespace="D-SI"/>
        <validate>elapsed &lt; mass</validate>
      </variable>
    </variables>
  </model>

  <algorithm namespace="ut.bad.Compare">
    <input  model="ut.bad.Mix" />
    <output model="ut.bad.Verdict" />
    <operations>
      <operation name="compare" returns="bool" />
    </operations>
  </algorithm>

  <model namespace="ut.bad.Verdict">
    <variables>
      <variable name="ok" type="bool" />
    </variables>
  </model>
</gorp>
